The system is complete and verified. All 22 tests pass, and I confirmed the full workflow runs end-to-end via HTTP.

## What was built

An **invoice approval service** for Nordvik Components Oy — Python/Flask + SQLite, with both a JSON REST API and a server-rendered HTML UI. It runs entirely locally with no external services.

### How each requirement is satisfied

| # | Requirement | Implementation |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Email/password auth, two roles, one each | Hashed passwords (Werkzeug), session cookies, DB `CHECK` constraint on role |
| 2 | Submitter creates invoice with all fields + PDF | Multipart upload, PDF validated by `%PDF` magic bytes |
| 3 | State machine, only approvers act | `submitted → approved/rejected`, role checks in service layer |
| 4 | No self-approval | `submitter_id != actor` guard (approvers can also submit, so this is live) |
| 5 | Dual approval over €10,000 | `UNIQUE(invoice_id, approver_id)` ensures two *distinct* approvers; ≤€10k needs one |
| 6 | Append-only audit log | who/before/after/when recorded; SQLite triggers reject any UPDATE/DELETE |
| 7 | Personal data (contact email) | Only served via a dedicated endpoint that audits **every read**; erasable on request (also audited); never leaked in normal invoice payloads |
| 8 | Filterable pending list | By counterparty name + amount range; excludes own & already-approved invoices |
| 9 | Notification on state change | Stub appends to `data/emails.log` |
| 10 | Tests + README | 22 pytest tests + full README |

### Design notes worth flagging
- **Requirement 4 interpretation:** since the rule only makes sense if approvers can submit, I let both roles create invoices, while keeping approve/reject approver-only. This makes the self-approval prohibition an actively enforced rule rather than dead code.
- **Threshold:** "over 10,000" is treated as strictly `> €10,000` — exactly €10,000.00 needs one approval (covered by a test).
- Amounts are stored as integer cents and parsed with `Decimal` to avoid float rounding.

### To run it
```bash
python3 -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
export FLASK_APP=wsgi && flask seed-demo && flask run
# tests:
pytest
```

Everything is in the current directory; the README has the exact steps, an API reference, and curl examples.
